---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
const img = (f: string) => `/homelab/kiwix/${f}.jpg`;
---
<Article
  eyebrow="Homelab · Guide"
  title="Offline Wikipedia on the home server"
  description="All of English Wikipedia, with pictures, is 119 GB. Put it on a server at home and it keeps working when the internet does not."
  date="October 2026"
  readingTime="5 min read"
>
  <p>All of English Wikipedia, pictures included, is 119 GB. That fits on the server
  in my basement with room to spare, and once it is there it keeps working when the
  internet does not. I added it in September as part of a plan for long outages,
  alongside the generator and a shelf of paper manuals.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src={img('01-library')} alt="The Kiwix library page listing the books on the server"
         width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The library page every device in the house can open: 22 books, no
    internet needed.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What this gets you</h2>

  <p>A website on your home network with Wikipedia, a dictionary, iFixit's repair
  guides, a medical encyclopedia, travel guides, open textbooks and some science
  video. Any phone or laptop in the house opens it in a browser, with no internet
  connection at all.</p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>An always-on machine with Docker.</strong> A home server, a NAS, a
    spare mini PC. The server software is light; the disk is the cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Disk space.</strong> My set is about 207 GB. Updates need room for
    two copies of the biggest book while the new one downloads, so plan on roughly
    240 GB free if Wikipedia is on your list.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://kiwix.org">Kiwix</a>.</strong> Kiwix packages a whole
    website as one <code>.zim</code> file, and <code>kiwix-serve</code> turns a folder
    of them into a website.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The steps</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Pick the books</h3>
      <p>Browse what exists in the <a href="https://library.kiwix.org">Kiwix
      library</a>. I skipped Khan Academy (168 GB, last built in 2023) and Project
      Gutenberg (206 GB). Mine:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Wikipedia (full, with images)</td><td>119 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CrashCourse (video)</td><td>21.5 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>TED science (video)</td><td>14.8 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>LibreTexts: nine subjects</td><td>about 12 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Math and Physics Stack Exchange</td><td>8.6 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wiktionary (no pictures)</td><td>8.5 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wikisource</td><td>8.0 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wikibooks</td><td>5.8 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>iFixit</td><td>3.3 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wikiversity</td><td>2.3 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MDWiki (medical)</td><td>2.1 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wikivoyage</td><td>1.0 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PhET simulations</td><td>0.1 GB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Download and verify</h3>
      <p>Every file on download.kiwix.org has a <code>.sha256</code> beside it. Check
      it. A 119 GB download that is quietly corrupt fails months later, at the moment
      you need it.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Run the server</h3>
      <p>Use the official image, <code>ghcr.io/kiwix/kiwix-serve</code>, with your
      books folder mounted read-only and <code>'*.zim'</code> as the argument. Keep
      the quotes (see below).</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Keep it current</h3>
      <p>I run a small script on the 2nd of every month. For each book it finds the
      newest file, skips it if I already have it, downloads to a <code>.part</code>
      file that can resume, checks the SHA-256, swaps it into place, deletes the
      older version, and restarts the container once at the end if anything changed.
      It also skips a book if the disk is too full and refuses to run twice at
      once.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Gallery
    items={[
      { src: img('02-library-phone'), alt: 'The library on a phone', label: 'Library' },
      { src: img('03-wikipedia'), alt: 'A Wikipedia article served offline', label: 'Wikipedia' },
      { src: img('04-ifixit'), alt: 'iFixit repair guides', label: 'iFixit' },
      { src: img('05-mdwiki'), alt: 'The offline medical encyclopedia', label: 'MDWiki' },
      { src: img('06-travel'), alt: 'Wikivoyage travel guide', label: 'Wikivoyage' },
    ]}
    cols={5}
    caption="The same books on a phone, over home wifi."
  />

  <h2>The parts that will bite you</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quote the glob.</strong> Pass <code>'*.zim'</code>, quotes included,
    so the glob is expanded inside the container, where the books are, instead of by
    the host's shell.</li>
    <li><strong>The full Wikipedia is published about every six months.</strong>
    Most months the updater finds nothing new for it, and that is normal.</li>
    <li><strong>The container name matters.</strong> My updater restarts the
    container by name, and the install template's default name is different. Under
    that name the restart is skipped, and the server keeps serving files the updater
    has already deleted. I renamed the container to match.</li>
    <li><strong>On Unraid, the flash drive cannot run scripts directly.</strong> It
    is FAT32 with no execute bit, so call the script with <code>bash</code>.
    Scheduled jobs go in a <code>.cron</code> file under the dynamix plugin folder,
    with no user field; a user field makes the job fail without an error.</li>
    <li><strong>There is no login.</strong> Keep it on your home network. Anyone who
    can reach it can read it, which is fine for Wikipedia and a reason to keep it
    off the internet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to check it worked</h2>

  <p>Open it on your phone over home wifi and search for something. Then unplug the
  modem and search again.</p>
</Article>
